[SPEAKER_02]: It's a symptom of an underlying problem.

190
00:14:38,133 --> 00:14:43,336
[SPEAKER_02]: These are symptoms of that disease that, you know, sometimes it's hot, sometimes it's... Yeah, yeah.

191
00:14:43,356 --> 00:14:45,717
[SPEAKER_02]: There's sharp pains, soft pains, numb.

192
00:14:45,777 --> 00:14:46,138
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

193
00:14:47,699 --> 00:14:49,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you're subjected to it.

194
00:14:50,240 --> 00:14:50,661
[SPEAKER_01]: You're in...

195
00:14:50,741 --> 00:14:51,561
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not healed, though.

196
00:14:51,661 --> 00:14:52,762
[SPEAKER_01]: You're imprisoned by it.

197
00:14:53,162 --> 00:14:56,124
[SPEAKER_01]: You're trapped within it, you're confined and so on.

198
00:15:00,127 --> 00:15:02,028
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't see that as a symptom,

199
00:15:03,333 --> 00:15:22,063
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, if you still feel justified and have the view that management of suffering, management of symptoms, doing something about it to overcome it when it arises, if you don't abandon that view and if you don't restrain yourself, you're not going to see how deep it goes, which means you're not going to see the source of it.

200
00:15:22,584 --> 00:15:23,604
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm aware of feeling.

201
00:15:24,285 --> 00:15:27,686
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm, in a way, aware of dukkha.

202
00:15:27,967 --> 00:15:30,468
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if you're aware of feeling correctly, if you're enduring the feeling,

203
00:15:33,302 --> 00:15:35,184
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that brings then the question, what is feeling?

204
00:15:36,104 --> 00:15:49,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Because most people would, again, through those views of management, proliferation and confusing information for understanding, would account on the level of our feeling at this, like, physical sensations that I have in my body.

205
00:15:49,815 --> 00:15:49,955
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

206
00:15:50,015 --> 00:15:51,096
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling is a state of mind.

207
00:15:51,216 --> 00:15:52,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling is always mental.

208
00:15:53,038 --> 00:15:54,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Bodily feelings are always mental.

209
00:15:57,281 --> 00:16:00,944
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's only three types of feelings on that level, which is the level of feeling.

210
00:16:01,943 --> 00:16:03,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Agreeable, disagreeable and neither.

211
00:16:04,025 --> 00:16:05,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Agreeable nor disagreeable.

212
00:16:05,667 --> 00:16:07,629
[SPEAKER_02]: Let's go to the second noble truth.

213
00:16:08,089 --> 00:16:12,013
[SPEAKER_02]: And this, monks, is the noble truth of the origin of dukkha.

214
00:16:12,914 --> 00:16:15,417
[SPEAKER_02]: The craving that makes for further being.

215
00:16:15,817 --> 00:16:16,158
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

216
00:16:16,318 --> 00:16:20,162
[SPEAKER_02]: Accompanied by passion, delight, seeking the light here and there.

217
00:16:20,502 --> 00:16:21,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Whose translation is that?

218
00:16:22,124 --> 00:16:22,264
[SPEAKER_02]: Eh...

219
00:16:22,364 --> 00:16:22,945
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not too sure.

220
00:16:23,792 --> 00:16:26,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the further being, I'm not sure about.

221
00:16:26,073 --> 00:16:26,373
[SPEAKER_02]: Anyway.

222
00:16:26,953 --> 00:16:28,634
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what is it then?

223
00:16:29,415 --> 00:16:31,515
[SPEAKER_01]: It brings us basically to the point I already said.

224
00:16:31,556 --> 00:16:35,617
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the origin of dukkha, the source of dukkha.

225
00:16:37,458 --> 00:16:45,982
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the basis, the necessary condition for presence of dukkha is presence of taṇha, is presence of craving.

226
00:16:47,803 --> 00:16:52,765
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not what you see, heard, smell, taste, touch, thought about, remembered, planned, failed,

227
00:16:53,880 --> 00:16:59,365
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about craving, taṇha, towards what's felt there and then.

228
00:17:00,125 --> 00:17:01,446
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the root of dukkha.

229
00:17:02,487 --> 00:17:07,191
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the world, saṃsara, is not, not the suffering.

230
00:17:07,972 --> 00:17:09,413
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the cause of suffering.

231
00:17:10,694 --> 00:17:14,177
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your attitude, that, on that emotional level, towards saṃsara.

232
00:17:14,337 --> 00:17:15,277
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what suffering is.

233
00:17:15,378 --> 00:17:16,899
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's your craving towards it.

234
00:17:17,339 --> 00:17:18,320
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what suffering is.

235
00:17:20,887 --> 00:17:21,447
[SPEAKER_01]: What craving?

236
00:17:21,467 --> 00:17:32,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, craving for more pleasure in pleasant feeling, craving for less pain in painful feeling, and craving for ignorance and ignoring and indolence in a neutral feeling.

237
00:17:32,171 --> 00:17:32,551
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

238
00:17:32,672 --> 00:17:35,773
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the root of the entire universe of suffering.

239
00:17:37,113 --> 00:17:49,398
[SPEAKER_01]: But in order to get onto the level of that root, you need to stop acting out of that craving, which is trying to manage your suffering, or pursue sensuality,

240
00:17:50,522 --> 00:17:55,406
[SPEAKER_01]: Or constantly being distracted through activities and avoiding boredom and solitude.

241
00:17:56,586 --> 00:18:09,556
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all these prerequisites in the form of lifestyle that the Buddha outlined, are basically just ways of rounding up that... that... that... towards the center of that suffering, which is your craving, which is that internal thing.

242
00:18:10,156 --> 00:18:18,322
[SPEAKER_01]: But if your lifestyle still revolves around, you know, sensuality, pursuing the further pleasure on account of pleasure that you felt,

243
00:18:19,160 --> 00:18:28,007
[SPEAKER_01]: Management your suffering, getting rid of acting out of pain, unwholesome acts of anger and retribution and all of that.

244
00:18:28,808 --> 00:18:35,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there's no way you're going to uproot suffering, craving taṇha mentally, when you're physically still... Passionately engaged.

245
00:18:35,673 --> 00:18:39,276
[SPEAKER_01]: Passionately engaged and maintaining that, maintaining it, keeping it alive.

246
00:18:40,793 --> 00:18:54,078
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first, you physically need to stop acting out of it, then verbally, then mentally, you can eventually see the roots, see your own attitude towards what's currently felt, and that's where the dharma is.

247
00:18:54,118 --> 00:18:58,260
[SPEAKER_01]: That what I said before, trying to get rid of it, resisting it.

248
00:19:01,737 --> 00:19:02,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Less of it or neither of it.

249
00:19:02,918 --> 00:19:04,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, wanting to switch it off.

250
00:19:04,479 --> 00:19:05,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Wanting to switch it off.

251
00:19:05,239 --> 00:19:05,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Wanting to change it.

252
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:11,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Wanting to sort of act out of it, because you feel actually confined within it.

253
00:19:11,703 --> 00:19:15,125
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to endure the confinement of the feeling and not act out of it.

254
00:19:15,685 --> 00:19:18,587
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to give up sensuality when it's pleasant.

255
00:19:18,787 --> 00:19:21,128
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to give management of pain when it's unpleasant.

256
00:19:21,268 --> 00:19:23,770
[SPEAKER_01]: And you need to give distraction and indolence when it's neutral.

257
00:19:25,331 --> 00:19:27,152
[SPEAKER_01]: And that will result in a sense of confinement

258
00:19:29,072 --> 00:19:41,144
[SPEAKER_01]: And the deep fatigue and resistance that we spoke before, but if you don't act out of it, the mind will calm down within that, and then you get to see for what it is.

259
00:19:41,164 --> 00:19:41,804
[SPEAKER_01]: That's wisdom.

260
00:19:42,165 --> 00:19:51,274
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a magical result of some method or some accidental insight or something.

261
00:19:52,495 --> 00:19:53,836
[SPEAKER_01]: Wisdom is just developed

262
00:20:02,462 --> 00:20:03,342
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop looking away.

263
00:20:03,902 --> 00:20:04,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop looking beyond.

264
00:20:05,723 --> 00:20:07,083
[SPEAKER_01]: You get to see what's right in front of you.

265
00:20:08,863 --> 00:20:09,963
[SPEAKER_02]: It removes that craving.

266
00:20:10,683 --> 00:20:13,644
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's what removes... you don't remove the craving.

267
00:20:13,804 --> 00:20:14,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I shall remove it.

268
00:20:14,804 --> 00:20:18,005
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you stop resisting it... You have to stop moving.

269
00:20:18,065 --> 00:20:18,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

270
00:20:19,165 --> 00:20:22,986
[SPEAKER_01]: When you get to see it for what it is, then craving will sort of evaporate.

271
00:20:23,486 --> 00:20:27,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Because craving requires you to want to look away, or more of it, or less of it.

272
00:20:27,906 --> 00:20:29,027
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you're maintaining it.

273
00:20:29,367 --> 00:20:29,667
[SPEAKER_02]: Right.

274
00:20:29,767 --> 00:20:31,467
[SPEAKER_02]: You remove it indirectly.

275
00:20:33,698 --> 00:20:34,379
[SPEAKER_02]: Not moving.

276
00:20:34,659 --> 00:20:35,840
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

277
00:20:36,020 --> 00:20:41,985
[SPEAKER_01]: By not providing a basis for craving to move, the craving will sort of dry up.

278
00:20:43,586 --> 00:20:45,888
[SPEAKER_02]: By not moving, by not trying to switch it off.

279
00:20:46,308 --> 00:20:48,750
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

280
00:20:51,252 --> 00:20:53,034
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the third noble truth.

281
00:20:53,334 --> 00:20:56,617
[SPEAKER_02]: This is the noble truth of the end of suffering.

282
00:21:00,099 --> 00:21:19,171
[SPEAKER_01]: Cessation of craving means cessation of possibility to suffer on account of anything that the senses, the aggregates, the world brings you.

283
00:21:20,172 --> 00:21:20,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Good or bad.

284
00:21:20,692 --> 00:21:21,233
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter.

285
00:21:21,533 --> 00:21:23,554
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot suffer, because you cannot crave.

286
00:21:26,550 --> 00:21:27,610
[SPEAKER_02]: You cannot be moved anymore.

287
00:21:27,811 --> 00:21:28,911
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot be moved, yeah.

288
00:21:33,573 --> 00:21:35,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Cooling down, dispassion, yeah.

289
00:21:35,394 --> 00:21:36,074
[SPEAKER_01]: Equanimity, yes.

290
00:21:36,154 --> 00:21:36,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Equanimity, yeah.

291
00:21:38,415 --> 00:21:41,337
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, the fourth noble truth is the truth of the way.

292
00:21:41,357 --> 00:21:45,459
[SPEAKER_01]: How to develop, yeah, how to develop that cessation, okay, of lethal path, yeah.

293
00:21:46,399 --> 00:21:46,959
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

294
00:21:47,119 --> 00:21:51,021
[SPEAKER_01]: The right, well, the right view first, always comes first.

295
00:21:51,041 --> 00:21:51,902
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to know, right?

296
00:21:51,922 --> 00:21:52,122
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

297
00:21:53,132 --> 00:21:54,973
[SPEAKER_01]: The root of the craving is not in the world.

298
00:21:55,793 --> 00:22:02,917
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not in insufficient management or not, you know, not a perfect technique or something else.

299
00:22:03,077 --> 00:22:04,998
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in the craving towards what I'm feeling.

300
00:22:05,078 --> 00:22:06,479
[SPEAKER_01]: My craving towards my feeling.

301
00:22:07,019 --> 00:22:07,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

302
00:22:08,901 --> 00:22:10,481
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first, I need to stop acting out.

303
00:22:10,681 --> 00:22:15,744
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the livelihood needs to start matching that principle you're trying to develop.

304
00:22:15,804 --> 00:22:17,545
[SPEAKER_01]: The speech needs to start matching the principle.

305
00:22:17,565 --> 00:22:19,066
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, your efforts need to start matching.

306
00:22:19,386 --> 00:22:20,467
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's noble as a path.

307
00:22:21,107 --> 00:22:22,748
[SPEAKER_01]: Right view, right mindfulness, right effort.

308
00:22:23,510 --> 00:22:31,120
[SPEAKER_01]: And everything else that follows, that results, then, eventually, in, like, complete immovability of the right kind, on the level of what's being felt.

309
00:22:33,342 --> 00:22:35,265
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, in the pleasure, there'll just be pleasure felt.

310
00:22:35,385 --> 00:22:41,132
[SPEAKER_01]: There will be no more craving, or, like, implicit craving for wanting more of it.

311
00:22:42,447 --> 00:22:42,928
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just gone.

312
00:22:43,068 --> 00:22:46,872
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's just to the extent the feeling's present, there's nothing more, there's nothing less.

313
00:22:46,952 --> 00:22:49,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're not resisting it, nor leaning towards it.

314
00:22:49,715 --> 00:22:50,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's not touching you.

315
00:22:51,417 --> 00:22:51,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

316
00:22:51,857 --> 00:22:52,878
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not yours anymore.

317
00:22:53,659 --> 00:22:57,183
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not being touched by it, which means it's not pressuring you anymore.

318
00:22:57,303 --> 00:22:58,905
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing wanted in regard to it.

319
00:23:09,997 --> 00:23:14,441
[SPEAKER_01]: So, pretty much, the Noble Eightfold Path is everything we've been describing from the beginning of this talk.

320
00:23:15,382 --> 00:23:20,207
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, oh, I need to understand one, two, three, and then give me these eight steps that I need to figure out.

321
00:23:20,707 --> 00:23:20,827
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

322
00:23:20,867 --> 00:23:26,813
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding one, two, three, is the eight steps, is the Noble Eightfold Path, is the Right View.

